{
  "name": "roundtrip-nanosecond-timestamp",
  "description": "Round-trip reconstruction with nanosecond-precision timestamps: append 5 durable events, save a snapshot at session sequence 3, reload, and confirm the reconstructed StoredSession matches exactly — the snapshot plus precisely the trailing events (sequences 4 and 5), with every envelope field and timestamp intact.",
  "captured_from": {
    "phase": "Phase 7 (persistence, Tasks 7.3/7.4)",
    "bug": "SqliteSessionStore first persisted snapshot timestamps as truncated unix-epoch *milliseconds* in the snapshots.timestamp column, losing nanosecond precision. The exact-match round-trip acceptance test (append N events + snapshot, reload, reconstructed StoredSession matches exactly) failed on reload because the loaded snapshot timestamp no longer equaled the saved one.",
    "fix": "The snapshots.timestamp column now stores the RFC3339 representation of the Timestamp (handle_save_snapshot in sqlite.rs) so the value round-trips losslessly; the store-level exact-match guarantee (shared by JsonlSessionStore, whose records round-trip through serde directly) holds for both backends.",
    "replay": "Apply the mutation transcript to a fresh store, load the session, and assert the reconstructed StoredSession equals expected_session exactly."
  },
